  "text": "Why did a traditional Danish wood workshop completely stop manufacturing wooden toys? In late nineteen forty-seven, the business acquired its first plastic injection machine. Employees installed the molding equipment in a building beside the woodworking factory. The firm began experimenting with plastic toy molding throughout nineteen forty-eight. In nineteen forty-nine, the company launched its plastic Automatic Binding Bricks. These early building bricks were initially sold exclusively within the Danish market. A molded plastic Ferguson tractor became their best-selling toy in nineteen fifty-one. In nineteen fifty-four, owner Godtfred Kirk Christiansen attended a British toy exhibition. A purchasing manager complained that the broader toy market completely lacked system. This striking feedback prompted Godtfred to assess the entire company product catalog. He carefully evaluated more than two hundred distinct company wood and plastic items. Godtfred realized that only building bricks fired imagination with unlimited creative potential. He spent months developing a unified system that challenged conventional ready-made toys. The company officially introduced the LEGO System in Play in nineteen fifty-five. The debut release centered on Town Plan Number One for urban play. The system guaranteed that elements purchased across different years fit together perfectly. Godtfred formulated six essential principles for retailers to understand this systematic concept. The toys had to remain compact, affordable, durable, and suitable for all ages. Retailers also received promotional town plan materials and in-store prize building competitions. Following the town launch, sales and corporate awareness of brick potential increased. In nineteen fifty-eight, designers patented the revolutionary hollow stud-and-tube coupling principle. Internal hollow tubes dramatically improved clutch power and overall model building stability. This patented mechanical coupling transformed the plastic brick into an interlocking foundation. The interlocking system offered endless combinations that standalone wooden toys could not. The System in Play and the new coupling principle became company cornerstones. In nineteen sixty, leadership formally decided to stop producing all wooden toys. They also phased out other non-system plastic toys from active factory production. The business concentrated all operations entirely on the interlocking plastic brick system. Focusing solely on the System in Play established the brand’s enduring global trajectory. Would the company have achieved this legacy without abandoning its original craft?",
